<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A SELECTED SPAN WITH NO MATERIAL (plan 53 §4.4 / A0b, QBX-178).

  THIS CASE GATES THE AUDIBLE AND THE NUMERIC HALF, NOT THE PICTURE. §4.4 says
  the span whose selected take has no material is drawn HATCHED, and a paint
  behaviour needs a PIXEL gate — that one is QBX-157's, which owns the compact
  column's backdrop and cut marks. What is gated here is everything beneath the
  pixels: the span plays SILENCE, and the ENVELOPE a painter would read over it
  is empty. A hatch drawn over a span whose envelope said "material" would be
  the real defect, and this is the assertion that forbids it.

  THE SHAPE, built out of two documented verbs rather than a fixture file. A 4 s
  sample is trimmed to 2 s — which CLAMPS take 0 to [0, 96000) — and then a
  second take is added at 96000, which GROWS the column back to 4 s without
  touching take 0. The result is a column NEITHER of whose takes covers it:
    take 0 — [0, 96000)
    take 1 — [96000, 192000)
  That matters: a column with one FULL take could not produce this case at all,
  because removing a take would simply fall back to a take that covers the
  span, which is correct and is not what §4.4 is about.

  THE MAP sends the second half to take 1, so before the removal both halves
  sound, each take reading the source from ITS OWN zero: 0.0667, 0.1764 twice.

  THE REMOVAL. `remove-take` takes take 1 away and the column does NOT shrink
  (A0a: a column keeps its length when a take leaves, which is what
  `duration_` is for). The map still names take 1 at 96000, so:
    - the stale index is out of range, so `activeTake_` is consulted — take 0
    - take 0 has NO MATERIAL at 96000
    - the answer is -1: SILENCE, with NO FALL-BACK (§3.1 step 5)
  and the one remaining take being PARTIAL is also invariant 3′: the column
  must NOT collapse to a plain cut, so `shape` stays "direct".

  WHAT BITES ON THE BASELINE, AND WHAT DOES NOT. Measured on main at a3aa1472
  — A0a's model, without A0b:

    - THE AUDIO ASSERTIONS ALL PASS THERE. The silence over the uncovered span
      is A0a's resolution rule, already green, and this case keeps them as
      REGRESSION COVER for it rather than as its own gate. Saying so matters:
      a reader who assumed the renders were what A0b added would think this
      case proves more than it does.
    - THE ENVELOPE ASSERTIONS FAIL, and they are A0b's. Both reads over
      [96000, 192000) come back with a full waveform — `[0]-50/50 [1]-50/51
      [2]-51/51 …` — where an empty envelope is expected, because the compact
      collect still delegates to the active take across the WHOLE column and
      so describes material the column does not play. That is plan 53's X10
      measured as numbers.

  `take=` IS NOT REJECTED ON THE BASELINE, IT IS IGNORED. `assert-envelope`
  declares its known attributes, so the runner WARNS ("unknown attribute(s)
  take … ignored") and carries on unless `SMARAGD_STRICT_ATTRS=1` — which
  means the `take=` assertion silently degrades into a second clip-mode read
  there. That is precisely why this case asserts the clip-mode envelope in its
  own right as well, instead of resting on `take=` alone: an attribute a
  baseline ignores proves nothing by itself.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="take_extents_hatch">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- Trim to 2 s. `add-sample` places a PLAIN clip, not a column — the
         column comes into existence with the first `add-take` below — so the
         trim is asserted through the clip's own window, which is all a plain
         clip can answer. -->
    <resize-clip clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="96000" srcStart="0"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="96000"/>

    <!-- ...and the new take grows the column back out without touching take 0. -->
    <add-take clip="0,0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" startOffset="0"
              index="-1" activate="0" at="96000" length="96000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        extents="0-96000;96000-192000" duration="192000"/>

    <set-comp-segment clip="0,0" at="96000" take="1"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="96000:1"
                        takeAtPos="0" expectTake="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" takeAtPos="96000" expectTake="1"/>

    <!-- BOTH halves sound, each take from its own zero. -->
    <render filename="teh_before.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="teh_before.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="teh_before.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="teh_before.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>

    <!-- THE TWO PICTURES, OVER THE SAME SPAN, WHILE BOTH TAKES EXIST. This is
         §4.4's subject stated as sharply as the suite can state it: over
         [96000, 192000) the COMP RESULT has material (take 1 carries it) while
         TAKE 0's own lane row has NONE (its extent ended at 96000) — and over
         [0, 96000) it is the other way round. A build that drew a take across
         the whole column cannot produce either answer.

         THESE are what `take=` exists for. The post-removal reads below are
         both empty, so they could not tell the per-take collect from the
         clip's; these can. -->
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" take="0" start="96000" length="96000"
                     width="64" expectEmpty="true"/>
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" take="1" start="0" length="96000"
                     width="64" expectEmpty="true"/>
    <!-- ...and the comp result over each of those spans is NOT empty, asserted
         as an identity against the take that does carry it, so neither read
         can be satisfied by an all-zero array. -->
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" take="1" start="96000" length="96000"
                     width="64" snapshot="teh_t1_second"/>
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="96000" length="96000" width="64"
                     compareTo="teh_t1_second"/>

    <!-- THE REMOVAL. -->
    <remove-take clip="0,0" take="1"/>
    <!-- Invariant 3′: one take, PARTIAL, so the column does not collapse. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="1" activeTake="0"
                        extents="0-96000" duration="192000"/>
    <!-- The selected span has no material. THE -1 IS THE WHOLE CASE. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" takeAtPos="96000" expectTake="-1"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" takeAtPos="0" expectTake="0"/>

    <render filename="teh_after.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <!-- The first half is untouched... -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="teh_after.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="teh_after.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <!-- ...and the span with no material is SILENT, in one assertion: there is
         nothing to tell apart inside it. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="teh_after.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="96000"
                         minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.005"/>

    <!-- THE ENVELOPE A PAINTER READS over that span, through the clip's own
         inline renderer — the same walk the arranger paints. The COMP RESULT
         has no material there... -->
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="96000" length="96000" width="64"
                     expectEmpty="true"/>
    <!-- ...and neither does the TAKE LANE's row for take 0, whose extent ends
         at 96000. `take=` reads collectTakeEnvelope, the exact call a take
         lane makes, which is the only way to tell the two pictures apart. -->
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" take="0" start="96000" length="96000"
                     width="64" expectEmpty="true"/>

    <!-- AND THE COVERED HALF IS NOT EMPTY, asserted as an IDENTITY rather than
         against hard-coded probes: over [0, 96000) the comp result IS take 0,
         so the clip's envelope and take 0's own must agree byte for byte. A
         windowing bug that zeroed too much would break this pair, and it
         cannot be satisfied by an all-zero array because the span is also
         asserted audible above. -->
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" take="0" start="0" length="96000" width="64"
                     snapshot="teh_take0"/>
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="0" length="96000" width="64"
                     compareTo="teh_take0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
